---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="Challenges with WebAssembly — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="The same Rust code runs in a browser tab. Getting it to compile was quick. Getting the colours right, reading files reliably and shipping it without breaking took me much longer."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-09-26">September 26, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>Challenges with WebAssembly</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          The same Rust code runs in a browser tab. Getting it to compile was
          quick. Getting the colours right, reading files reliably and
          shipping it without breaking took me much longer.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 7 min read</p>
      </header>

      <aside class="lp-tldr">
        <p class="lp-tldr-label">TL;DR</p>
        <p>
          The <a href="/app.html">web version</a> of LightPhotos is the
          desktop app's Rust code, compiled to WebAssembly so it runs in a
          browser tab. It works in desktop Chrome and Edge. Almost none of the
          hard bugs showed up when I compiled it. They showed up when it ran,
          in the clock, threads, crashes, colours and file access, and each
          one needed its own workaround, a few of which changed the desktop
          app too.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <h2>What I learned</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Getting it to compile didn't tell me much.</strong> The
          first time the app ran in a browser it hit three bugs, and the
          compiler couldn't have caught any of them.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Writing my predictions down helped.</strong> My plan got
          memory right and threading completely wrong. I only know that
          because I wrote the plan down before I started.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>In the browser, a crash in any library is my
          problem.</strong> When Rust code panics (crashes on purpose because
          something unexpected happened) in the browser, it kills the worker.
          So I have to check a file before handing it to a decoder that might
          panic on it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>How I deploy matters as much as the code.</strong> Two
          files from the same build were cached for different lengths of
          time, and that broke every photo for anyone who had visited
          recently.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How the browser version is laid out</h2>
      <p>
        The browser version is really two programs. The main one runs the
        window, the interface and the graphics on the page's main thread. A
        second, smaller one runs in Web Workers, which are background threads
        the browser gives you, and it does the slow work of decoding RAW files
        and rendering exports. I needed the second program because a worker
        has no window, so it can't run the interface or the graphics code.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 214" role="img" aria-label="The browser build. The page's main thread runs the interface and draws with WebGPU. It reads and writes your folder through the File System Access API. It sends decode and export jobs to up to four Web Workers, each a separate copy of a smaller WebAssembly program with its own memory. Downloads: about 3.2 MB for the main program and 1.1 MB for the worker, compressed.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">ONE BROWSER TAB</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="0" y="28" width="250" height="72" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="16" y="50">Main thread</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="16" y="68">interface, catalog, WebGPU drawing</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="16" y="86">12 MB wasm, 3.2 MB compressed</text>

          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="250" y1="64" x2="300" y2="64"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M300 59 L310 64 L300 69 Z"></path>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="256" y="56">jobs</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="310" y="28" width="330" height="116" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="326" y="50">Up to 4 Web Workers</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="326" y="62" width="70" height="30" rx="4"></rect>
